{"parentUuid":"a9382e16-6cc9-4f36-8bd5-c355a930545c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a3dd1dc86014a90af","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gk2YgwfXjYCyPjJbkiFxjM","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"learning to branch\" inference latency overhead per node GNN CPU GPU SCIP slowdown\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Learning to Compare Nodes in Branch and Bound with Graph Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2022/file/cf5bb18807a3e9cfaaa51e667e18f807-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Optimizing High-Throughput Inference on Graph Neural Networks at Shared Computing Facilities with the NVIDIA Triton Inference Server | EPJ Research Infrastructures | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s41781-024-00123-2\"},{\"title\":\"CoDL: Efficient CPU-GPU Co-execution for Deep Learning ...\",\"url\":\"https://chrisplus.me/assets/pdf/mobisys22-CoDL.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Low-latency Mini-batch GNN Inference on CPU-FPGA Heterogeneous Platform | DeepAI\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/low-latency-mini-batch-gnn-inference-on-cpu-fpga-heterogeneous-platform\"},{\"title\":\"Accelerating Mobile Inference Through Fine-Grained CPU-GPU Co-Execution | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-16345-5_4\"},{\"title\":\"A Unified CPU-GPU Protocol for GNN Training | Proceedings of the 21st ACM International Conference on Computing Frontiers\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/10.1145/3649153.3649191\"},{\"title\":\"PERFSAGE: GENERALIZED INFERENCE PERFORMANCE PREDICTOR FOR\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2301.10999\"},{\"title\":\"From packets to predictions on GPU: Accelerated graph-based intrusion detection system - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1389128625009193\"},{\"title\":\"[2206.08536] Low-latency Mini-batch GNN Inference on CPU-FPGA Heterogeneous Platform\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.08536\"},{\"title\":\"$$\\\\nu$$ GNN: Non-Uniformly partitioned full-graph GNN training on mixed GPUs | CCF Transactions on High Performance Computing | Springer Nature Link\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42514-025-00224-3\"}]\n\nBased on my search results, I found relevant information related to your query about \"learning to branch\" and inference latency with GNNs in the context of SCIP.\n\nThe first result is a NeurIPS 2022 paper titled \"Learning to Compare Nodes in Branch and Bound with Graph Neural Networks,\" which uses GPU (Nvidia Tesla V100) and CPU (Intel Xeon Gold 6126) for training and evaluation. However, the search results don't provide specific quantitative data on the inference latency overhead per node or slowdown measurements.\n\nThe remaining results cover related topics about GNN inference latency, including:\n\n- Significant synchronization overhead required to combine partial results when using heterogeneous CPU-GPU execution\n- Overhead associated with CPU-GPU data transfers \n- Task scheduling approaches that hide CPU-FPGA data communication overhead to reduce inference latency\n\nHowever, the search results don't contain specific information about the inference latency overhead per node for \"learning to branch\" methods or quantified slowdown factors when using GNNs on CPU vs GPU in the SCIP solver context. To find the specific latency metrics you're looking for, you may need to access the full paper directly or search for more recent benchmark results from that research area.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"56316075-8fc9-4662-af88-cfc6cce439d3","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:46.004Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a9382e16-6cc9-4f36-8bd5-c355a930545c","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"e96450f7-7987-4a55-b1aa-6549d56aa33a","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a3dd1dc86014a90af","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SbcX2XjzvTjYpAReK8dhk7","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"graph neural network constraint satisfaction problem variable ordering symmetric layout small training data\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Frontiers | Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction\",\"url\":\"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/artificial-intelligence/articles/10.3389/frai.2020.580607/full\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Variable Ordering Heuristics for Solving Constraint Satisfaction Problems - ADS\",\"url\":\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2019arXiv191210762S/abstract\"},{\"title\":\"[1912.10762] Learning Variable Ordering Heuristics for Solving Constraint Satisfaction Problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.10762\"},{\"title\":\"Learning variable ordering heuristics for solving Constraint Satisfaction Problems - ScienceDirect\",\"url\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0952197621004255\"},{\"title\":\"Learning Variable Ordering Heuristics for Solving Constraint Satisfaction Problems | DeepAI\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/learning-variable-ordering-heuristics-for-solving-constraint-satisfaction-problems\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction - PMC\",\"url\":\"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7959828/\"},{\"title\":\"Benchmarking Graph Neural Networks in Solving Hard Constraint Satisfaction Problems\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.18419\"},{\"title\":\"[1909.08387] Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.08387\"},{\"title\":\"(PDF) Learning Variable Ordering Heuristics for Solving Constraint Satisfaction Problems\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/338137671_Learning_Variable_Ordering_Heuristics_for_Solving_Constraint_Satisfaction_Problems\"},{\"title\":\"Graph Neural Networks for Maximum Constraint Satisfaction Jan Toenshoﬀ\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1909.08387\"}]\n\nBased on the web search results, here are the key findings related to your query about graph neural networks applied to constraint satisfaction problems with considerations for variable ordering, symmetric layout, and small training data:\n\n## Graph Neural Networks for Constraint Satisfaction\n\nMany combinatorial optimization problems can be phrased in constraint satisfaction problems, and graph neural network architectures have been introduced for solving such optimization problems.\n\n## Variable Ordering in CSPs\n\nBacktracking search algorithms are often used to solve Constraint Satisfaction Problems, and the efficiency of backtracking search depends greatly on variable ordering heuristics. Graph Neural Networks have been used to represent the internal states of search processes, which can effectively capture relationships between variables.\n\n## Small Training Data and Scalability\n\nA key finding relevant to your interest in small training data is that training is unsupervised and it is sufficient to train on relatively small instances; the resulting networks perform well on much larger instances (at least 10-times larger). Additionally, networks trained on small instances achieve good results on much larger inputs.\n\n## Architecture Overview\n\nTo solve Max-CSPs, a GNN is trained which is viewed as a message passing protocol that is executed on a graph with nodes for all variables and edges for all constraints of the instance.\n\nThe research shows that GNNs with unsupervised learning can effectively handle constraint satisfaction problems while generalizing from small training sets to larger problem instances.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"dfdd12bf-8d18-4c3c-a4b0-a2d2283d7207","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:47.125Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e96450f7-7987-4a55-b1aa-6549d56aa33a","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
{"parentUuid":"455c0cc2-bfea-4b8d-a94a-4eb5bfca70ef","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"56e72661-9c70-4718-ac89-40d9fd2179ff","agentId":"a3dd1dc86014a90af","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RcyKxH2ck1nWRW7N4aA3gA","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Mirhoseini chip placement controversy reproducibility 2024 followup nature retraction\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"AlphaChip (controversy) - Wikipedia\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AlphaChip_(controversy)\"},{\"title\":\"Nature flags doubts over Google AI study, pulls commentary – Retraction Watch\",\"url\":\"https://retractionwatch.com/2023/09/26/nature-flags-doubts-over-google-ai-study-pulls-commentary/\"},{\"title\":\"Reevaluating Google’s Reinforcement Learning for IC Macro Placement – Communications of the ACM\",\"url\":\"https://cacm.acm.org/research/reevaluating-googles-reinforcement-learning-for-ic-macro-placement/\"},{\"title\":\"‘Doing good science is hard’: retraction of high-profile reproducibility study prompts soul-searching\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-024-03178-8\"},{\"title\":\"The False Dawn: Reevaluating Google’s Reinforcement Learning for Chip Macro Placement\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2306.09633v10\"},{\"title\":\"Ending an Ugly Chapter in Chip Design - IEEE Spectrum\",\"url\":\"https://spectrum.ieee.org/chip-design-controversy\"},{\"title\":\"The False Dawn: Reevaluating Google's RL for Chip Macro Placement | Hacker News\",\"url\":\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=36501194\"},{\"title\":\"More Details, but Not Enough – Communications of the ACM\",\"url\":\"https://cacm.acm.org/news/more-details-but-not-enough/\"},{\"title\":\"Google’s problems with reproducibility | Statistical Modeling, Causal Inference, and Social Science\",\"url\":\"https://statmodeling.stat.columbia.edu/2022/05/03/googles-problems-with-reproducibility/\"},{\"title\":\"RETRACTED ARTICLE: AI system outperforms humans in designing floorplans for microchips\",\"url\":\"https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-021-01515-9\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of the Mirhoseini chip placement controversy and its 2024 follow-up developments:\n\n## Overview of the Controversy\n\nOn 21 September 2023, Andrew B. Kahng's accompanying News & Views article was retracted; the retraction notice said that new information about the methods used in the Google paper had become available after publication and had changed the author's assessment, and it also said that Nature was conducting an independent investigation of the paper's performance claims.\n\n## 2024 Follow-Up Developments\n\nBy late September 2024, the editor's note was removed without explanation, but Nature published an addendum to the original paper (dated 26 September 2024). However, the addendum addressed some methodological details but still lacked the full training and evaluation inputs needed for independent replication.\n\n## Reproducibility Status\n\nThe core issue remains unresolved: No positive independent replications of the Nature results have been reported in peer-reviewed literature three and four years since publication. Additionally, as of 2026 no such results have been published on publicly available benchmarks.\n\n## Scientific Integrity Concerns\n\nIn October 2024, sixteen methodological concerns were grouped into categories and itemized as \"initial doubts\" in a detailed critique by chip design researcher Igor L. Markov in Communications of the ACM. The critique described multiple questionable research practices in the evaluation of AlphaChip, particularly around selective reporting of benchmarks and outcomes (cherry-picking), selective use of metrics, and selective choice of baselines.\n\nAs of 2026, this paper was prefaced with an ACM \"EXPRESSION OF CONCERN: An investigation is underway regarding the content and transparency of disclosure for this article.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"0359aadc-5dd5-4c22-a8c0-c824fbf70e48","timestamp":"2026-05-07T20:06:49.399Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"455c0cc2-bfea-4b8d-a94a-4eb5bfca70ef","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"D:\\claude pj\\zmd","sessionId":"7db7f276-a2bf-4762-b9b8-bb35f8cf3fb9","version":"2.1.128","gitBranch":"master","slug":"squishy-zooming-sphinx"}
